     - a dict `{hook name: entry}`, the entries as in any `hookeds.json` (`event`, `handler`, `timeout_ms`, `condition`, `every_ms`, `config`, ...);
-    - `std.hooks()`: the hooks of the `agent-fsm` template (`std.hooks("eca-claude")` / `("eca-pi")` for those); they are read from `$AOS_HOME/templates/<template>/hookeds.json` and copied in, so recompiling follows a changed template;
+    - `std.hooks()`: the hooks of the `agent-fsm` template (`std.hooks("eca-claude")` / `("eca-pi")` for those); they are read from `$AOS_HOME/templates/agents/<template>/hookeds.json` and copied in, so recompiling follows a changed template;
     - a Python function marked `@fn.hook("event", ...)` (see "Functions as tools and hooks" below);
     - a list of those, laid one over the other (a later entry with the same name wins, `None` drops one): `hooks=[std.hooks(), {"timer": None, "mine": {...}}]`; but two hook groups (`std.hooks(...)` of two templates, the `hooks` of `defs.load`) that give one name with different entries are `SpecBad` unless a dict of yours after them names it;
     - or `defs.extern("../shared/hookeds.json")`: `hookeds.json` becomes a link (see "extern" below; the whole field, not an item of the list).
